<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sverre of Norway</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1150–1202</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Sverre Sigurdsson, Sverre Sigurdsson of Norway</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sverre Sigurdsson was King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> from 1184 to 1202, having led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/birkebeiner">Birkebeiner</a> faction in the civil wars from 1177. Claiming to be a son of King Sigurd Munn, he defeated his rival <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnus-erlingsson">Magnus Erlingsson</a> and strengthened royal power against the church, which excommunicated him. His career is told in Sverris saga, written partly under his own direction.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sverre-of-norway.jpg" alt="Sverre of Norway" width="222" height="255" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Alexander Bugge (1870–1929) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sverre_Sigurdsson_(Nidaros_Cathedral).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>According to Sverris saga, Sverre was born to Gunnhild and her husband Unås, a comb maker from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faroe-islands">Faroes</a>. The family moved there when he was five, and he was raised in the household of Unås's brother Roe, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of the Faroes, and ordained a priest. In 1175 his mother told him that his father was King Sigurd Munn, and the next year he went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>.</p><p>Historians have questioned this paternity. Some think the claim false, as did many of Sverre's contemporaries, but most hold that the question cannot be answered; he consistently refused an ordeal by fire to prove it. In January 1177 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/birkebeiner">Birkebeiners</a>, a rebel party, were crushed at the Battle of Re, and Sverre agreed to lead their remnant, which numbered no more than seventy men according to the saga.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Sverre was proclaimed king at Øretinget in June 1177. For years his band was almost constantly on the move against King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnus-erlingsson">Magnus Erlingsson</a> and his father Erling Skakke. Erling was killed at the Battle of Kalvskinnet on 19 June 1179, and Sverre secured Trøndelag. He built a large fleet, taking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bergen">Bergen</a> in 1183, and had the huge ship Mariasuda built. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-fimreite">Battle of Fimreite</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sognefjord">Sognefjord</a> on 15 June 1184 Magnus drowned and his party, the Heklungs, was broken, leaving Sverre sole king.</p><p>His tactics were mobile: he fought in smaller groups instead of a shield wall and often directed his men from horseback. Further risings followed, among them the Kuvlungs, whose leader Jon Kuvlung was killed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bergen">Bergen</a> in 1188, and the Øyskjeggene, beaten at Florvåg on 3 April 1194.</p><p>A more serious breach came with the Church. Archbishop Eirik Ivarsson fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> in 1190, and Pope Celestine III's letter of 15 June 1194 backed the archbishop, who then excommunicated Sverre. In 1198 Innocent III placed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> under interdict. A papal letter annulling the excommunication that Sverre produced in 1197 is held by most historians to have been forged. In 1196 the church-supported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bagler">Baglers</a> rose against him in a war that outlasted him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Sverre died on 9 March 1202. Many consider him one of the most important rulers in Norwegian history. Sverris saga, written in part under his sponsorship, is the main source for his life and is probably biased; papal and episcopal letters offer an alternative on church affairs, and the two mostly agree on the hard facts. A speech against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> written by someone close to him argued that his excommunication was unjust and defended his right to appoint bishops.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sverre of Norway” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>From 1130 to 1240 the kingdom was torn by civil wars between rival claimants and factions such as the Birkebeiner and the Bagler; <b>Sverre Sigurdsson</b> secured his throne with a sea victory at Fimreite in 1184.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-norway">Kingdom of Norway · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In a naval battle, the Birkebeiner-backed <b>Sverre Sigurdsson</b> defeated King Magnus Erlingsson, who was killed, and took the Norwegian throne.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-15">15 June in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1178 King <b>Sverre of Norway</b> defeated the Jämts and made them pay tax, and Jämtland became part of the Kingdom of Norway, though it belonged to the Swedish archdiocese of Uppsala.</blockquote><a href="/state/jamtland">Jämtland</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bergen">Bergen</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/norway">Norway</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=59.93,10.68&amp;year=1183" aria-label="Norway on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Sverre of Norway"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5455 4969h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5455 4969h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=59.93,10.68&amp;year=1183">What was here in 1183? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
